        <li><strong><a href="/tiny-homes-on-wheels-texas/">Tiny homes on wheels</a></strong> for owners who want to follow work, family or the next piece of land.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>San Antonio ADU and Tiny House Rules: What to Know</h2>
      <p>San Antonio allows accessory dwelling units in many residential zoning districts, which is a big reason casitas are so popular here. The city's development code sets standards for an ADU's size relative to the main home, height, setbacks, parking and how many units can share a lot, and those standards can differ by zoning district, historic district or neighborhood conservation district. Rules have been updated over the past few years, so always verify the current requirements with the City of San Antonio Development Services Department before you commit to a design.</p>
      <p>Outside city limits the picture changes. Unincorporated Bexar County and the surrounding counties generally have no zoning, but subdivision deed restrictions, septic permitting, floodplain rules and utility availability still apply. A tiny home on wheels is usually treated as a recreational vehicle, which can make it easier to place in an RV park but harder to live in full time on a city lot.</p>
      <div class="callout"><p><strong>Our feasibility study does this homework for you.</strong> Before design starts, Cowboy Tiny Homes checks zoning, setbacks, easements, floodplain maps and utility connections for your exact address. For the bigger statewide picture, read our guide to <a href="/guides/tiny-house-laws-texas/">tiny house laws in Texas</a>.</p></div>

      <h2>Designing for South Texas Heat and Hill Country Ground</h2>
      <p>Summers in San Antonio regularly push past 100°F, and a small home heats up fast if it is designed poorly. We orient windows to limit west sun, specify deep porch overhangs, use radiant-barrier roof decking and continuous insulation, and size ductless mini-split systems for the actual square footage instead of over-sizing. A metal or light-colored roof can make a measurable difference in comfort and power bills.</p>
      <p>The ground matters too. North and west of the city you hit limestone and caliche close to the surface, which affects foundation design, trenching for utilities and septic layout. On the east and south sides, clay soils move with moisture and call for a properly engineered slab or pier-and-beam foundation. We account for both in the site plan.</p>

